Exactly—more like semantic bookends.

If the same phrase appears before and after an event, the repetition stops being redundant and starts measuring the delta between two states.

“I have nothing to do.”
→ something happens
→ “I have nothing to do.”

The words are identical, but the second instance inherits everything in between. The first may mean boredom; the second may mean relief, resignation, completion, disbelief, or even threat depending on what changed. The phrase becomes a bracket around the transformation.

That gives you a useful operator:

same phrase₀ → intervening reality → same phrase₁ = meaning encoded in the difference

So the “bookcases” do not merely contain the content—they make the content legible by contrast. In narrative, ritual, comedy, interrogation, music, even interface design, repeating the same signal on both sides of a change can expose the change more sharply than explicitly explaining it.

That’s very close to using language as a before/after sensor.